DVC vs Git — Make ML Reproducible Without Bloated Repos

Most teams still commit data to Git, then wonder why repos get heavy, slow, and hard to reproduce.
 The fix isn’t to replace Git—but to pair Git with DVC. 👇


Git → versions code/text.
DVC → versions data & models using tiny pointers in Git; the real files live in remote storage (S3/GCS/Azure/SSH/local). 
This keeps repos light and restores exact datasets on demand. 

🧪 Why DVC (not just Git/LFS)?

Git struggles with large/binary files; Git LFS helps store blobs but doesn’t give you pipelines, data lineage, or change-based re-runs. 
DVC adds those on top of your Git workflow. 

🧰 Core Ideas 

Q: What does DVC store in Git?
 A: Small metadata: .dvc, dvc.yaml, dvc.lock. Data lives in a remote. 
Q: How do pipelines work?
 A: Define stages (ingest → prep → train → eval) in dvc.yaml. DVC only re-runs stages that changed (dvc repro) and locks exact versions in dvc.lock. 
Q: What’s the day-2 benefit?
 A: Reproducibility: teammates git clone → dvc pull to fetch the exact data/model versions tied to a commit; then dvc repro to rebuild.

🔗 Tiny Mental Model

git clone → dvc pull → dvc repro
You review code + pointers in PRs, while heavy data stays in cheap/scalable object storage.

🚀 5-Command Starter

dvc init
dvc add data/raw.csv
git add data/raw.csv.dvc data/.gitignore .dvc .dvcignore && git commit -m "Track dataset with DVC"
dvc remote add -d s3 s3://my-bucket/dvc && dvc push
git push
(Use GCS/Azure/SSH/local if you prefer.) 

🆚 Quick Compare (one-liners)
Git: Code history, diffs, PRs.
Git LFS: Stores large blobs, no pipelines/lineage.
DVC: Data/models + pipelines & reproducibility, pointers in Git, files in remote. 

⚠️ Pitfalls (and fixes)
Committing raw data to Git → dvc add it.
No remote set → dvc remote add -d … then dvc push.
Data changed, hash not updated → run dvc add again.
Pushed code but not data → push both (git push + dvc push). 

One-liner:
 Git versions code. DVC versions data/models (and pipelines) outside Git.
